analysis:
Examined the SSDPrefixCache.swift head excerpt (lines 1839-2184) and the change map. The only production change is evictOldestEntry at head lines 1773-1778. It replaces an inline loop over index.oldestEntries() with SSDOwnedEntryRetirement.evictOldest(index:) and a callback to retireIndexedEntries. The hasSafeRoot guard, the per-URL retireIndexedEntries ownership/epoch path and the eviction stats remain. The unchanged sweep, startup scan, settleDurableDonation and performIndexedRemoval paths keep the T-041 controls: ownsEvictionRoot gating, symlink and safe-root checks, binding validation and the TTL.

The new test fixtures in SSDOwnedEntryRetirementTests head lines 37-247 assert the intended behavior:
- external loss or a symlink substitution stops eviction and reconciles without following the link (lines 117-142);
- a refused epoch deletes nothing (lines 187-196);
- busy and undeletable files are skipped (lines 84-115);
- the fallback snapshot is bounded even if the callback mutates the index (lines 198-247).

SSDBlockIndexTests covers oldestFirstSnapshot ordering and isolation. No TTL, budget, naming, AAD or crypto change was observed, so the T-041 / SEC-035 posture is unchanged.

Remaining contract to check in integration: the SSDOwnedEntryRetirement.evictOldest and SSDBlockIndex.oldestFirstSnapshot implementations (likely in SSDOwnedEntryRetirement.swift and SSDBlockIndex.swift) were not in this batch. Verify that evictOldest:
- returns 0 on externalChange, as the old loop did;
- terminates within the snapshot bound;
- does not hold the index lock during the callback.

The tests indicate this behavior. A regression there would affect availability or disk-budget handling (T-016 / T-041 budget), not confidentiality.
